WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.110
My name's Jeff Kember and I do
come from the visual effects

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:04.610
industry.

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:07.320
I was most recently
a CD supervisor,

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.280
and I speak VFX and cloud.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.710
So really happy
to be here today.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:16.460
I'm going to be giving a talk
on Google Cloud Storage options

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:17.640
and just what it all is.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.090
When I joined Google,
I wasn't quite exactly

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:22.600
100% clear on all the
different storage options

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.710
and how they worked
and why you'd use them.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:26.168
I'm also going to
talk a little bit

00:00:26.168 --> 00:00:30.590
today about getting your data
to the cloud and how that looks.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:35.900
So from a Google Cloud
Storage options standpoint,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.150
Google Cloud Storage
is a giant block store.

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.270
So if you have
onset data-- let's

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:44.010
say you've gone and shot a
huge amount of imagery onset

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.710
and you want to
put it somewhere,

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:47.710
the standard storage is
a great place to put it.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:51.520
It's super-low latency, it's
almost limitless in size,

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.790
and it's $0.026 a gig so
it's really inexpensive.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:58.820
We have a less expensive option
for $0.02, which is the Durable

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:00.530
Reduced Availability Storage.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:02.460
So what does that mean
to the VFX community?

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.420
That means that the latency
is increased a tiny bit.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:08.007
That's a really great place
to park a significant amount

00:01:08.007 --> 00:01:09.590
of data that you
want to be able to do

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:11.030
read-and-write operations for.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:14.055
If you want to be able to copy
this data to a file system,

00:01:14.055 --> 00:01:16.302
if you want to be able
to copy it to local SSD

00:01:16.302 --> 00:01:18.510
as on VMs, which we will
talk about in a few minutes,

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.010
if you actually want to do
transcoding or rendering

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:24.360
work loads against this, DRA is
a great place to put the data.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.860
We'll also talk a little later
about Nearline storage, which

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:31.360
is our archiving solution,
and go forward from there.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.629
As far as storage on
the actual machines go,

00:01:35.629 --> 00:01:37.920
when you spin up a VM, you
have a few different choices

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.140
on how you want to get the data
available to the machine you

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:43.180
want to run it on.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.780
So this slightly
complicated diagram here

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.660
talks about persistent disk.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.160
PD stands for persistent disk.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.710
It's network storage,
and it's available

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:54.020
both with enterprise-level
spinning disk

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:55.380
as well as with SSD.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.770
And we configure them
in 10-terabyte sizes.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.980
We also have local SSD
attached to the machines,

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.240
and that is next generation.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:05.480
It's super fast.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.170
Think of it less
as a SATA option

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:08.900
and more of a PCIE option.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:14.020
It's almost 700,000 IOPS and
it's 1.5 terabytes of capacity.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:16.500
So that's crazy fast.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.160
Instead of thinking
of it as fast disk,

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.640
we think of it more
as slow memory.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.600
It's about the speed
of DDR2 memory.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.900
So if you want to deliver
something like a ZFS file

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.430
system, for instance,
you can spin up a VM,

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:31.960
throw a ZFS on it, attach 1.5
terabytes of storage to it,

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:37.670
and now you're serving up 1.6 to
1.7 terabytes of data at memory

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.310
speeds, at line rates.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.600
That's about 8 gig per
second on the file system.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.750
You can also attach 10 terabytes
of PD to that, as well.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.060
So that's a really
meaty little file system

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.344
that you can put up,
which is open source,

00:02:51.344 --> 00:02:52.760
and you're only
paying for the VM.

00:02:55.282 --> 00:02:56.740
If you have raw
camera data and you

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.550
want to perform transcode
operations, for instance,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:02.670
on it, you can store it on PD.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:04.740
You can also copy it
over to local SSD.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.340
So the use cases
I've identified here

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.860
are transcoding, fluid
simulations, Nuke, ZFS,

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.020
Read Cache.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.030
So we have customers
who want to do

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:14.390
fluid simulation on the cloud.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:16.530
And from a storage
standpoint, they'll

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.590
copy the alembic data
and the scene data over,

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:23.490
they'll do the Naiad or Bifrost
or RealFlow fluid sim on it.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:27.080
But they'll use the
super-high I/O capabilities

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.960
of that machine to be able
to write all the cache data.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:34.026
So the point is that with local
SSD you can get really high ops

00:03:34.026 --> 00:03:36.150
and you don't have to worry
about using any network

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.360
bandwidth to do it.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:40.540
I've had as much as 30%
of my simulation time

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:43.780
be eaten up by copying
files across the network

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.037
to an Isilon.

00:03:45.037 --> 00:03:46.370
Not that the Isilon wasn't fast.

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:48.940
It was just, that's 20
to 40 gigs per frame

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:53.320
of data you're moving, and that
does impact simulation times.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:56.091
So from a network
connection standpoint,

00:03:56.091 --> 00:03:58.590
we've talked significantly about
how many points of presence

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.700
Google has and how strong
the Edge network is.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.340
The bigger issue is, as visual
effects companies and media

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.890
companies, how do you get
your data onto the cloud.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:09.525
So we have several
different options.

00:04:09.525 --> 00:04:12.470
We have customers who
light up dark fiber

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:16.390
between their location and
a peering location for us.

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:20.500
We have customers who will work
with a partner such as Sohonet,

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.550
where they'll buy a
10-gig interconnect.

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:25.147
And they'll only pay for one
gig to the outside world.

00:04:25.147 --> 00:04:27.230
But they'll be able to get
a much higher bandwidth

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:31.077
connection to Google and just
pay a fractional increase,

00:04:31.077 --> 00:04:32.660
not what the full
load would be if you

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:35.180
wanted to have that go
to the outside world.

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:37.550
Google also has a
VPN offering, which

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:39.640
is a fully managed
solution, which can

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:41.270
deliver gigs of connectivity.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:42.920
The nice thing
about that solution

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:46.460
is, you just tell us what
you Juniper or Cisco box you

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:48.530
have on premises, or other.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:50.920
And we have a profile
that works for it.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:51.600
It's managed.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:52.440
It just works.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.820
And if you want to run
commercial or private queuing

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:58.560
software, that all
works over the VPN.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:02.130
It's just an extension
to your network.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.610
So as far as the
transcoding workload goes,

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:08.100
so the green represents
you're on premises or on set.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:11.170
And this is a copy-to-Google
cloud storage.

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:13.010
So you put in the
punch ECS, and you

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:14.180
can park all the data there.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.330
When you want to run a
transcode workload on it,

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:20.690
you can spin up VMs using
FFmpeg, just open source.

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:23.955
We can also work with
PandaStream and Zencoder.

00:05:23.955 --> 00:05:27.330
A local SSD is really the
secret sauce for this.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.400
Because of the
signature of the job,

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:32.510
there's a huge amount of
random reads and writes

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:33.800
that occur in a transcode.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:38.310
So you can spin up a 8-, 16-,
32-core VM and run multiple

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:39.790
transcodes in parallel.

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:42.150
And then you can write
all that data out to disk,

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:44.790
and the local SSD will
handle that for you.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:46.797
If you want to run
more, we have customers

00:05:46.797 --> 00:05:48.380
who are using a
product like Deadline.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:53.280
Or you can use pipeline
effects, Cube or Tractor,

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.630
or your own queuing system.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:58.600
The cool part about that
is that the queuing system

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:01.370
will spin up the VMs,
copy the data from GCS

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:05.210
over to the local SSD, unpause
the job, run the transcode,

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:06.810
and then, after
the job's finished,

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:10.210
copy all the data back
to GCS, which is here,

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:11.620
and then spin the VMs down.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.390
So you only pay
for what you use.

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:15.040
You can go massively
parallel with it.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:18.740
We've had customers who
have 5,000 hours of content

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:20.850
that they want to transcode.

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.930
In this example,
it was all German.

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:25.555
And then they needed to be able
to deliver it for distribution.

00:06:25.555 --> 00:06:29.240
And they were used to having
a 7- to 12-day delivery cycle.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.470
And we said to them, well,
how many hours you have?

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:32.640
And we got the file
sizes and figured

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.020
what the longest transcode was.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:37.689
We were able to deliver
the job in just over a day.

00:06:37.689 --> 00:06:38.980
That was the longest transcode.

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:40.600
So we just ran it all
parallel, all at once.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:41.630
And it was the
same cost to them,

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:43.660
whether it would be just
over a week or a day.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:45.560
That was one of the
nice things about that.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:47.960
So you've parked it
on DRA at this point.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:50.910
So it's on Durable Reduced
Availability Storage.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:55.210
So it's the same system as
GCS, but again, slightly higher

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:55.710
latency.

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:57.050
But you've already
done your copy,

00:06:57.050 --> 00:06:58.466
you've already
done the transcode,

00:06:58.466 --> 00:06:59.557
you've put it back to DRA.

00:06:59.557 --> 00:07:01.640
At this point, you can
push it out to the network.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:03.431
You can use one of our
partners like Fastly

00:07:03.431 --> 00:07:04.530
if you need to use a CDN.

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:07.206
If you just want to deliver
it to another provider

00:07:07.206 --> 00:07:09.830
and they're going to handle the
distribution, this is your out.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:11.996
Now, you still want to, of
course, retain this data.

00:07:11.996 --> 00:07:13.680
So now you can put
it on Nearline.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.470
So the magic part with Nearline
is, it's $0.01 per gig.

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:20.140
So again, on the
far left-hand side,

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:25.420
we've gone from GCS at
$0.026 to DRA at $0.02 a gig,

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:27.640
and again, as much reading
and writing as you want.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:29.473
And then, when you move
it over to Nearline,

00:07:29.473 --> 00:07:33.200
that becomes a $0.01 per
gig place to store it,

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:36.190
and it can live there as
long as you want it to.

00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:38.350
If you wish to read
that data back,

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:40.840
it's going to cost
you $0.01 to do so.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:44.310
So the nice thing about
this is that the data

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:46.800
is available to you
almost instantly.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.430
The latency from first read to
byte is two to five seconds.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:54.510
So it's not hours,
it's just seconds.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:56.310
It comes back extremely quickly.

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:58.700
I'll have a little slide with
some more details on that.

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:01.700
But we have customers who will
put their data in Nearline

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:03.830
and they'll store it there.

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:06.402
If they wish to do operations
on it, instead of being worried

00:08:06.402 --> 00:08:08.110
about how many times
I'm going to read it

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:10.577
and how many times do I get
charged that one-cent fee,

00:08:10.577 --> 00:08:12.660
they just flip the switch
and move it back to DRA.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:14.510
And it sits in DRA
and they perform

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:18.120
all the reroute operations
on it for weeks or months,

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:18.740
potentially.

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:20.910
I realize I can't
give historical data,

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:22.700
as Nearline is still
very new for us.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:24.790
But those are the use cases that
we are using, moving forward

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:25.290
with it.

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:30.050
So the compute engine
we're running on

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:32.330
is really exciting
for me in the fact

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:35.070
that it's crazy-high
performance.

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:37.610
You are only paying
for what you use.

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:42.039
The real differentiator for us
is the local SSD performance

00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:45.030
for the transcode, for
the simulation workloads.

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:48.010
From a storage standpoint,
this previous transcoding slide

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:49.910
that I had, it's
the same work case

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:52.280
if you want to run Nuke
jobs, for instance.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:55.210
If you've already rendered the
image in the cloud, it's there.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:57.520
And you parse the Nuke
file when you submit it

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:59.202
through your scheduler.

00:08:59.202 --> 00:09:00.660
Only the elements
that have changed

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:01.760
are going to be uploaded.

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:05.110
And you're able to benefit
from the super-high-I/O output

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:09.170
of the local SSD drives that are
physically attached to the VMs.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:11.700
So we have customers who
are using Nuke on this.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:13.565
We have customers who
are using FluidSIMs.

00:09:13.565 --> 00:09:14.940
They have, for
their proprietary,

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:17.720
Fire systems that run
on top of our systems.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:20.700
And again, all of them are using
this really high-I/O storage.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.740
And what it does for them is
saves their on-premises file

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:24.770
system.

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:29.400
I'm sure if anyone has run
large rendering systems, where

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:31.640
you have tens of
thousands of cores

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:34.610
and you have a significant
amount of compute happening,

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:37.660
if you load your file system
up with a large amount of I/O

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:40.901
for a FluidSIM or any
other type of simulation

00:09:40.901 --> 00:09:43.400
or compositing workload with
deep data, where you're looking

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:47.690
at an order of magnitude
more data per element,

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:49.530
you're in a scenario
where that file system

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:50.530
performance can degrade.

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:53.210
And then you start
starving out your CPUs.

00:09:53.210 --> 00:09:54.970
And as soon as that
happens, you have

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:58.200
to start throttling one and
favoring another and such.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:00.900
If you move the high-I/O
workloads to Google Cloud

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:03.510
Platform, you don't have to
worry about your file system

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:04.510
performance on premises.

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:07.290
You can focus on having
100% of your machines

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:09.070
running renders or
a smaller percentage

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:11.570
running comp and sim
jobs, but you're not

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:14.340
negatively impacted by that.

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:17.950
So as far as Nearline
storage as a slide goes,

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:20.590
this details a little bit more
of the relevant information.

00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:25.270
So it's $0.01 per gig to store
it, which is super inexpensive.

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:26.800
It's one gig when you read it.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:28.800
So again, if you want to
read it more than once,

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:31.650
or if you really intend to
be doing operations on it,

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:33.160
you want to put it
on DRA at least,

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:35.109
or GCS, depending on
your latency needs.

00:10:35.109 --> 00:10:36.900
We have customers who
are excited that, OK,

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:38.350
hear the $0.01 and
they're like well, OK,

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:39.510
so everything's $0.01 now.

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:41.780
You're like, well,
no, it's really

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:43.500
$0.02 if you want to
do operations on it

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:45.774
and $0.01 if you want
to park it somewhere.

00:10:45.774 --> 00:10:47.940
There is a one-month minimum
charge to put it there.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:50.310
So it's priced in such a
way that it is not intended

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:51.880
for production workloads.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:55.150
But it's priced in
such a way that it's

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:58.180
intended to be used for
ultra-long-term storage.

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:00.560
Another nice aspect of
being able to use Nearline

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.379
as an archiving
tool versus tape is,

00:11:03.379 --> 00:11:05.420
you're going to make two
to three copies on tape,

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:07.090
and then you have to
transport them and store them,

00:11:07.090 --> 00:11:09.120
and you had to pay for the
robot and someone to run it,

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.250
and then you have to
track and manage and deal

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:12.280
with all of this.

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:15.920
The nice thing is you
can modify the size

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:19.270
of the footprint of the data
that you've put on Nearline.

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:20.780
So if you take a
400-terabyte show

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:22.440
and you need to
put it somewhere,

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:24.350
then you can throw
it onto Nearline.

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:26.990
And then you can put a pipe
technical assistant on it

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:30.750
to go through and look a shotgun
and figure out what can stay

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:32.360
and what doesn't have to.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:34.800
And you can reduce
the size of the show.

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:36.920
I've had shows that
were 400-plus terabytes

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:40.660
and we've reduced them down
to 225 terabytes on disk,

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:42.530
because of course we
didn't backup the temp

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:43.160
files, obviously.

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:44.701
But you go through
and you figure out

00:11:44.701 --> 00:11:46.460
you don't need 17
versions of Character X.

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:50.450
And you don't need a whole
bunch of temporary MARI archives

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:52.502
that were 250 gigs apiece.

00:11:52.502 --> 00:11:53.960
You can go through
and say, we only

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:55.450
use these two and
these were live

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:56.866
and these are the
latest versions.

00:11:56.866 --> 00:12:00.080
So the ability to change
the footprint of the archive

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:03.390
really affects the long-term
storage cost as well as

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:05.500
the organization of the project.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:08.800
So you can see it's
super-high durability.

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:10.060
The retrievals are fast.

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:12.440
They're only a couple
seconds to get the data back.

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:14.670
I'm sure everyone here
has done restores or made

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:15.770
restore requests.

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:18.190
And you've waited anywhere
from a couple of hours

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:20.420
to go and get that tape
and load it in the robot

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:24.055
and restore a 20-gig
character archive.

00:12:24.055 --> 00:12:26.430
And then you realize, oh,
well, that texture was actually

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:28.500
borrowed from over here so you
have to do a second pass to get

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:29.230
the data back.

00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:31.315
Or you restored a
significantly larger portion

00:12:31.315 --> 00:12:32.940
than what you needed
because you wanted

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:34.340
to make sure you got it all.

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:35.275
The nice thing
with this solution

00:12:35.275 --> 00:12:36.890
is you can traipse through
the file structure,

00:12:36.890 --> 00:12:38.515
grab the files you
need, and just bring

00:12:38.515 --> 00:12:39.920
them online right now.

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:42.830
And the fact that
the more you store,

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:44.080
the higher the throughput is.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:45.720
So those customers
with large pipes,

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:48.442
if they have hundreds of
terabytes or petabytes of data

00:12:48.442 --> 00:12:50.150
store, they have a
significant throughput

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:50.940
and it's predictable.

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:52.731
And they know what the
cost is going to be,

00:12:52.731 --> 00:12:56.550
and our APIs are known.

00:12:56.550 --> 00:13:00.670
From a pricing standpoint, both
the compute and the storage

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:03.940
have been heading
down with Moore's Law.

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:08.310
So you can see our
curve from 2006 to 2015.

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:11.740
It's continuing to
go in that direction.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:15.856
So customers who've been using
our Ivybridge technology,

00:13:15.856 --> 00:13:18.910
we've rolled out Haswell
in a number of our zones,

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:21.680
and we're continuing to update
and refresh our hardware.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:24.590
In that context, you're able to
get the increased performance

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:26.670
at not the same cost
but at a lower cost,

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:28.930
as we continue to
reduce our costs,

00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:33.910
as well as increase our storage
capacities and availability.

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:38.750
So from just an overall use case
standpoint, with Google Cloud

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:41.490
Storage most customers
here are going

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:45.215
to be putting their data on
GCS from an upload standpoint

00:13:45.215 --> 00:13:46.344
and parking it there.

00:13:46.344 --> 00:13:48.010
And then they're going
to be spinning up

00:13:48.010 --> 00:13:49.700
VMs as file systems.

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:52.200
And they're going to use ZFS,
or they're going to use Avere,

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:53.850
or they going to use Gluster.

00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:55.040
Avere is here to talk today.

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:57.610
They're a fantastic solution
that most everyone in the room

00:13:57.610 --> 00:13:58.450
knows about.

00:13:58.450 --> 00:13:59.500
They have it on prem.

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:02.470
Our cloud offering is excellent.

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:04.490
You can have
between 3 and 30 VMs

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:06.450
and it can scale
larger than that.

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:09.030
You get to use our fast,
low-latency network fabric,

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:12.170
and you're able to use our
persistent disk SSD based

00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:14.000
for the back-end storage.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:16.950
So we are able to serve
up file systems that

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:19.840
are capable of having tens
of thousands or hundreds

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:22.000
of thousands of cores point
at them simultaneously

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:24.440
and be able to deliver that
throughput that you need

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:25.750
at a really high read rate.

00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:28.650
It's also very nice to be able
to have something like Avere,

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:31.470
in the fact that you can use it
as simply a caching appliance.

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:32.680
So you don't have
to pre-populate it

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:33.554
if you don't want to.

00:14:33.554 --> 00:14:35.770
You can spin it up
and read through it.

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:37.970
And then the data is cached.

00:14:37.970 --> 00:14:39.440
Your hit rates are high.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:42.470
For smaller jobs
that are potentially

00:14:42.470 --> 00:14:47.800
hundreds of machines, you can
point at a ZFS file system.

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:51.680
And Bjorn Leffler, the engineer
who did our ZFS file system

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:53.740
is here today to
answer any questions

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:55.070
you may have about that.

00:14:55.070 --> 00:14:58.250
He's in the blue shirt at the
back of the room waving hello.

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:00.610
It's exciting in
the fact that it

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:03.070
can stand up to hundreds
of machines hitting it.

00:15:03.070 --> 00:15:05.070
It'll deliver eight gig
per second all day long.

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:08.330
And as I mentioned before,
it's backed by our local SSD.

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:10.609
You can also have
multiple ZFS file systems.

00:15:10.609 --> 00:15:12.150
You have your texture
on one, and you

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:13.787
can have your alembic
data that churns

00:15:13.787 --> 00:15:15.620
a little bit on the
other, and you can point

00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:16.859
multiple mount points at it.

00:15:16.859 --> 00:15:18.400
So feel free to come
and talk with me

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:22.130
after, or Bjorn, about
some of the different VFX

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:23.984
workloads for storage.

00:15:23.984 --> 00:15:25.400
Happy also to
answer questions you

00:15:25.400 --> 00:15:28.330
might have about getting
the data to the cloud.

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:31.759
Just to wrap up here,
putting the data on GCS,

00:15:31.759 --> 00:15:33.550
spinning up a file
system to be able to run

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:34.780
compute loads against it.

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:37.412
Once you've completed
your compute loads,

00:15:37.412 --> 00:15:38.787
if you were on a
file system, you

00:15:38.787 --> 00:15:40.670
can put it back
on GCS to park it,

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:43.439
or you can copy the local
SSD data back to GCS.

00:15:43.439 --> 00:15:45.230
And then you can spin
your file system down

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:47.370
if you don't have
production workloads to run.

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:50.240
And finally, you can use our
network to deliver the data.

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:51.940
And then, once the
show's wrapped,

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:55.070
you can take the data as
you've been finaling the shots,

00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:57.610
you can move it off to
Nearline, and the entire show

00:15:57.610 --> 00:16:01.380
can sit on Nearline for
as long as you want it to.

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:04.870
Yet you can bring any assets
back online in seconds,

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:05.820
to be able to get at.

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:08.660
And I think everyone can agree
that that's a really useful use

00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:12.180
case for storage, being able
to get at a show from six

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:16.750
or eight years ago and be able
to pull those assets online.

